  "query_preview": "..\n\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Widespread smoke continues to degrade air quality and \n visibility, with Northwest Montana currently seeing the worst \n impacts. \n\n-  Elevated to near-critical fire weather continues across \n Southwest Montana through Monday. Wind gusts up to 30 mph are \n expected around Dillon Monday afternoon. \n\n-  A gradual cooling trend arrives by the middle of next week. A \n disturbance late Wednesday into Thursday will bring rain and \n thunderstorm chances, primarily along the Continental Divide. \n\nThe Northern Rockies will remain under the influence of a dry\nwesterly flow aloft through early next week. Regional wildfire\nsmoke has settled heavily across the area, particularly across\nNorthwest Montana where visibility at the Kalispell airport has\nremained near 2 miles. Smoke is expected to remain anchored in \nthe valleys through the afternoon, though we may see some \nimprovement in visibility and air quality by Monday as winds begin\nto increase. \n\nMonday remains our primary fire weather concern. A subtle feature\nwill move through the area, slightly increasing winds while\nrelative humidities remain quite low. Southwest Montana will be\nthe focal point for this concern, particularly around Dillon,\nwhere afternoon wind gusts up to 30 mph are forecast. This will\ncreate near-critical fire weather conditions across the Southwest,\nwith a few isolated spots in the higher terrain potentially\nreaching Red Flag criteria. Winds are expected to diminish on\nTuesday, though dry and warm conditions will persist. \n\nA gradual cooling trend will continue through the remainder of the\nweek. By Wednesday, a disturbance will make its way into the\nregion, bringing a noticeable wind shift\u2014starting out of the north\nin Northwest Montana before transitioning across the majority of\nthe area by Thursday. This system will also bring widespread\nchances for precipitation and thunderstorms late Wednesday into\nThursday. The best probabilities for beneficial moisture will be\nfocused along the Continental Divide, with Marias Pass having the\nhighest chances. Because this precipitation will be primarily\nconvective, amounts will be hit-and-miss. The highest potential\nfor measurable rain favors Northwest, West Central, and Southwest\nMontana, while Idaho will likely see very little. \n\nBy Friday and into next weekend, the focus for thunderstorm\nactivity will shift south into Southwest Montana as the region\ntransitions into a southwesterly flow pattern.",
